  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gradually warming temperatures today and Sunday \nwith increasing precipitation chances each day. \n\nSurface high pressure shifts northeast towards New England\ntoday and tomorrow, with gradually warming temperatures\nexpected. High temperatures will be in the 70s to mid 80s across\nthe forecast area. Aloft, multiple shortwave troughs track\nacross the mid-atlantic, bringing daily precipitation chances\nmainly along and west of the Blue Ridge. Precipitation chances\nincrease Sunday, although coverage of showers and thunderstorms\nremains scattered due to weak forcing.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Returning to a summer-like pattern next week.\n\nBeginning of next week, ensemble model guidance supports an UL \ntrough continuing to dig in to our region, leading to falling \nheights and increasing chances for showers and t-storms Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday morning. With Tuesday's dew points in the mid \n70s to upper 60s and high temperatures in the mid 70s west of \nthe Blue Ridge and high 80s east of the Blue Ridge, there is \npotential for ample moisture and heat to support showers and \nt-storm development. This coincides with ample instability \nrising into the 1,500 to 2,500 J/kg range. With enough vertical \nshear and height falls, global models support an active day of \nthunderstorm activity. Ensembles show some sagging of the \nsurface isobaric pattern with its position indicative of a lee- \nside trough. This will likely be one focus for convection along \nwith terrain circulations and the upstream cold front. \n\nDamaging wind gusts and torrential downpours are possible with any \nsuch storm. Given the later arrival of the front, the threat for \nstorms may persist beyond the core diurnal heating hours. \n\nModels show this frontal zone sagging southward to near the Virginia-\nNorth Carolina border by mid-week. A shift to post-frontal \nnorthwesterlies should gradually quell the threat for thunderstorms. \nDew points begin to fall with temperatures remaining seasonable. \nTuesday's hot temperatures seem to persist into Wednesday through \nFriday, with high temperatures largely topping out in the upper 80s \nto low 90s which is close to climatology for late July.  Slightly \nlowered dew points allows for Overnight lows fall into the 60s most \nnights. This subtle decrease in humid conditions aids lowering \nprecipitation chances to around 20% that could persist through the \nrest of the week.",
